  <!-- ═══════════ INTRODUCTION ═══════════ -->
  <h2 id="introduction">Introduction</h2>

  <p>
    Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) have advanced remarkably in recent years, evolving
    from static image captioning to general-purpose systems capable of processing video, audio,
    and text together.<d-cite key="fu2024mme"></d-cite> These models are increasingly being
    deployed in real-world settings &mdash; from healthcare consultations to educational
    assessments to legal proceedings &mdash; where understanding requires interpreting not just
    words, but <em>tone of voice</em>, <em>facial expressions</em>, <em>timing</em>, and
    <em>context</em> simultaneously.
  </p>
  <p>
    Consider how hiring works today. A candidate records video responses to structured interview
    questions. An AI system scores their performance based on speech content, vocal patterns, and
    visual cues. Similar AI-driven video assessments are expanding into telemedicine triage,
    courtroom analysis, and parent-teacher evaluations. As these systems move into high-stakes
    decisions, two questions become critical: <em>How reliable are they? And do they treat
    everyone fairly?</em>
  </p>
  <p>
    This blog presents <strong>SONIC-O1</strong>, a comprehensive benchmark designed to answer
    both questions. We evaluate state-of-the-art MLLMs on real-world audio-video interactions
    across 13 conversational domains, testing not just <em>what</em> models understand, but
    <em>when</em> events happen and <em>how fairly</em> they perform across demographic groups.
  </p>

  <h3 id="challenges">Challenges</h3>
  <p>
    Despite rapid progress, evaluating MLLMs on audio-video understanding remains difficult.
    Existing benchmarks exhibit two critical gaps:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Audio is treated as optional.</strong> Most video benchmarks either omit audio
      entirely or replace it with text transcripts and
      subtitles,<d-cite key="ataallah2025infinibench"></d-cite><d-cite key="wang2025lvbench"></d-cite>
      discarding paralinguistic cues like hesitation, emphasis, and emotional tone that are
      essential for understanding real communication.</li>
    <li><strong>Demographic fairness is not assessed.</strong> Even when native audio-video inputs
      are evaluated, group-wise analysis across demographic groups is largely
      absent,<d-cite key="chen2024cgbench"></d-cite><d-cite key="li2025omnivideobench"></d-cite>
      making it impossible to determine whether model performance varies systematically by race,
      gender, or age.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Together, these gaps leave a fundamental question unanswered: <em>Can current MLLMs operate
    accurately and equitably in real-world deployment?</em>
  </p>

  <h3 id="existing-benchmarks">Existing Benchmarks</h3>
  <p>
    The table below compares SONIC-O1 against prominent video-QA and audio-visual benchmarks.
    While several benchmarks address individual aspects of multimodal evaluation, none combine
    native audio-video QA, full temporal localization, demographic metadata for fairness analysis,
    and open-source availability:
  </p>
  <figure>
    <iframe src="./diagrams/benchmark-comparison.html" width="100%" height="560" scrolling="no" style="border: none; overflow:hidden;"></iframe>
    <figcaption>Table 1: Benchmark comparison. SONIC-O1 is the first to combine all key capabilities. Use filters to compare subsets.</figcaption>
  </figure>


  <!-- ═══════════ SONIC-O1 ═══════════ -->
  <h2 id="sonic">SONIC-O1</h2>

  <p>
    To address these limitations, we introduce <strong>SONIC-O1</strong> (<b>SO</b>cial
    <b>N</b>atural <b>I</b>nteraction <b>C</b>orpus, <b>O</b>mnimodal v<b>1</b>), the first
    open-source, fully human-verified benchmark for real-world audio-video understanding.
  </p>

  <div style="display:flex; justify-content:center; gap:2.5rem; flex-wrap:wrap; margin:1.5rem 0; padding:1.25rem 2rem; background:var(--vi-gray-50); border:1px solid var(--vi-gray-200); border-radius:8px;">
    <div style="text-align:center;"><div style="font-size:1.6rem; font-weight:800; color:#E10098; line-height:1.2;">231</div><div style="font-size:0.75rem; color:var(--vi-gray-500); text-transform:uppercase; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Videos</div></div>
    <div style="text-align:center;"><div style="font-size:1.6rem; font-weight:800; color:#E10098; line-height:1.2;">~60h</div><div style="font-size:0.75rem; color:var(--vi-gray-500); text-transform:uppercase; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Duration</div></div>
    <div style="text-align:center;"><div style="font-size:1.6rem; font-weight:800; color:#E10098; line-height:1.2;">4,958</div><div style="font-size:0.75rem; color:var(--vi-gray-500); text-transform:uppercase; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Human-Verified QAs</div></div>
    <div style="text-align:center;"><div style="font-size:1.6rem; font-weight:800; color:#E10098; line-height:1.2;">13</div><div style="font-size:0.75rem; color:var(--vi-gray-500); text-transform:uppercase; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Domains</div></div>
    <div style="text-align:center;"><div style="font-size:1.6rem; font-weight:800; color:#E10098; line-height:1.2;">3</div><div style="font-size:0.75rem; color:var(--vi-gray-500); text-transform:uppercase; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Tasks</div></div>
    <div style="text-align:center;"><div style="font-size:1.6rem; font-weight:800; color:#E10098; line-height:1.2;">6</div><div style="font-size:0.75rem; color:var(--vi-gray-500); text-transform:uppercase; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Demographic Groups</div></div>
  </div>

  <p>
    We collected 231 videos from YouTube restricted to CC BY 4.0 licenses, filtered from an
    initial pool of 2,237 candidates through license verification and manual quality screening.
    All annotations were drafted using Gemini 2.5 Flash, then <em>fully reviewed, corrected,
    and verified</em> by a team of 5 domain experts. Each video segment is tagged with perceived
    demographic attributes &mdash; race, gender, and age &mdash; enabling systematic fairness
    evaluation.
  </p>

  <figure>
    <img src="assets/teaser-figure.png" alt="SONIC-O1 benchmark overview" style="width: 100%; max-width: 850px; margin: 0 auto 1rem auto; display: block; border-radius: 4px;">
    <figcaption>Figure 1: SONIC-O1 overview. Three evaluation tasks with demographic metadata for fairness-aware assessment across 13 conversational domains.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3 id="domains">Domains &amp; Data</h3>
  <p>
    SONIC-O1 spans <strong>5 high-impact domains</strong> and <strong>13 conversational
    topics</strong>, selected to represent real-world interactions where AI errors carry
    tangible consequences:
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img src="assets/sunburst-topics.png" alt="13 topics across 5 domains" style="width: 100%; max-width: 600px; margin: 0 auto 1rem auto; display: block; border-radius: 4px;">
    <figcaption>Figure 2: From job interviews to emergency response &mdash; 13 real-world conversational domains across professional, educational, legal, service-oriented, and public health settings.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Videos range from 30 seconds to 60 minutes, stratified across short (&lt;5 min), medium
    (5&ndash;20 min), and long (20&ndash;60 min) to assess robustness across durations.
  </p>

  <h3 id="tasks">Three Evaluation Tasks</h3>
  <p>
    SONIC-O1 evaluates three complementary capabilities. Each targets a different dimension of
    audio-video understanding: can the model grasp the <em>gist</em>, reason about <em>specific
    details</em>, and pinpoint <em>when</em> events occur?
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Task 1 &mdash; Video Summarization</strong> (231 instances): Generate a narrative
      summary of a full video (up to 60 minutes). Tests <em>global comprehension</em>.</li>
    <li><strong>Task 2 &mdash; Multiple-Choice QA</strong> (1,335 instances): Answer a 5-option
      MCQ per 3-minute segment, including a &ldquo;Not enough evidence&rdquo; option, with a
      supporting rationale. Tests <em>fine-grained reasoning</em>.</li>
    <li><strong>Task 3 &mdash; Temporal Localization</strong> (3,392 instances): Given an anchor
      event, predict the start and end timestamps of a target event. This is the most challenging
      task &mdash; it tests whether models can identify not just <em>what</em> happened but
      <em>when</em>.</li>
  </ul>
  <figure>
    <img src="assets/task-distribution.png" alt="Question distribution" style="width: 100%; max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto 1rem auto; display: block; border-radius: 4px;">
    <figcaption>Figure 3: Distribution of questions across topics and tasks.</figcaption>
  </figure>


  <!-- ═══════════ EVALUATION & RESULTS ═══════════ -->
  <h2 id="results">Evaluation &amp; Results</h2>

  <p>
    We evaluated six state-of-the-art MLLMs on SONIC-O1: the closed-source
    Gemini 3.0 Pro<d-cite key="deepmind2025gemini3"></d-cite> and five open-source models &mdash;
    Qwen3-Omni<d-cite key="xu2025qwen3omni"></d-cite> (30B),
    UniMoE-2.0<d-cite key="li2025unimoe2"></d-cite> (33B),
    MiniCPM-o-2.6<d-cite key="yao2024minicpm"></d-cite> (9B),
    VITA 1.5<d-cite key="fu2024vita"></d-cite> (8B), and
    VideoLLaMA2<d-cite key="cheng2024videollama2"></d-cite> (7B).
    All models were chosen because they natively support audio-video inputs.
  </p>

  <h3 id="main-results">Overall Performance</h3>
  <figure>
    <iframe src="./diagrams/results-table.html" width="100%" height="500" scrolling="no" style="border: none; overflow:hidden;"></iframe>
    <figcaption>Table 2: Performance across three tasks. &dagger; = closed-source. Macro-averaged across 13 topics.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    <strong>Gemini 3.0 Pro</strong> leads across all three tasks: 96.4% MCQ accuracy, 7.07
    summarization score, and 25.4% R@0.5 on temporal localization. Among open-source models,
    <strong>Qwen3-Omni</strong> (30B) is the strongest &mdash; trailing Gemini by just 2.8% on
    MCQ but by a substantial 22.6% on temporal localization, revealing where the real gap lies.
  </p>
  <p>
    Model scale plays a clear role. The jump from 7B (VideoLLaMA2: 54.3% MCQ) to 30B
    (Qwen3-Omni: 93.6%) is dramatic. However, UniMoE-2.0 at 33B does not outperform the smaller
    Qwen3-Omni, suggesting that architecture and training recipe matter as much as raw parameter
    count.
  </p>

  <h3 id="temporal-challenge">The Temporal Gap</h3>
  <p>
    The most striking finding: even the best model achieves only <strong>25.4% R@0.5</strong>
    on temporal localization, compared to <strong>96.4%</strong> on MCQ. Open-source models
    collapse below 3%. For every 100 temporal events, they correctly localize fewer than 3.
    High-stakes domains like emergency response and mental health counseling are hit
    hardest &mdash; exactly the scenarios where precise timing matters most.
  </p>
  <div class="key-finding insight">
    <div class="finding-label">Key Finding</div>
    <strong>Models know what happened but not when.</strong> Content understanding and temporal
    reasoning are fundamentally different capabilities. The 22.6-point gap between Gemini and
    Qwen3-Omni on temporal localization dwarfs the gap on any other task.
  </div>
  <figure>
    <iframe src="./diagrams/domain-performance.html" width="100%" height="700" scrolling="no" style="border: none; overflow:hidden;"></iframe>
    <figcaption>Figure 4: Per-domain performance. Switch tasks via dropdown; click legend items to toggle models.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Structured interactions (job interviews, medical consultations) are easiest for all models.
    Emotionally complex scenarios (mental health counseling, emergency response) prove
    significantly harder, even for Gemini. Perceptual complexity and emotional nuance remain
    challenges beyond language understanding alone.
  </p>

  <h3 id="modality-ablation">Effect of Audio</h3>
  <p>
    Does adding audio to video input actually improve performance? We tested this through an
    ablation study comparing video-only versus video+audio input.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <iframe src="./diagrams/modality-ablation.html" width="100%" height="450" scrolling="no" style="border: none; overflow:hidden;"></iframe>
    <figcaption>Figure 5: Modality ablation. Green deltas show improvement from adding audio; red indicates degradation.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    The answer is <strong>yes &mdash; but only if the model is large enough</strong>.
    Qwen3-Omni gains +10.7% MCQ accuracy and +2.2 summarization points with audio. But
    VideoLLaMA2 (7B) actually <em>degrades</em> on summarization (&minus;0.1), suggesting
    that effective audio-visual fusion requires sufficient model capacity.
  </p>

  <h3 id="duration-robustness">Duration Robustness</h3>
  <figure>
    <iframe src="./diagrams/duration-analysis.html" width="100%" height="540" scrolling="no" style="border: none; overflow:hidden;"></iframe>
    <figcaption>Figure 6: Performance by video length. Switch tasks via dropdown.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Performance degrades as videos get longer, with temporal localization most affected. Gemini
    drops from 50.6% to 18.6% R@0.5 between short and long videos &mdash; a 32-point decline.
    MCQ accuracy is more stable, but smaller models still fall significantly. Sustained event
    tracking over long-form content remains a key bottleneck.
  </p>


  <!-- ═══════════ FAIRNESS ═══════════ -->
  <h2 id="fairness">Fairness Analysis</h2>

  <p>
    A benchmark is incomplete without fairness evaluation. SONIC-O1&rsquo;s demographic metadata
    enables systematic analysis across race, gender, and age &mdash; revealing persistent
    disparities. Explore all 9 combinations below:
  </p>
  <figure>
    <iframe src="./diagrams/fairness-heatmap.html" width="100%" height="380" scrolling="no" style="border: none; overflow:hidden;"></iframe>
    <figcaption>Table 3: Fairness analysis &mdash; 3 demographics &times; 3 tasks. Green = best group, red = worst. Data from Tables 4&ndash;6 of the paper.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    <strong>Race</strong> shows the largest disparities. In temporal localization, Gemini achieves
    40.9% R@0.5 for Indigenous participants but only 19.5% for Black participants &mdash; a
    21-point gap. Qwen3-Omni collapses to 0.0% for Indigenous participants entirely.
    <strong>Gender</strong> and <strong>age</strong> effects are smaller but consistent: female
    participants and the 40+ age group score slightly higher across models, likely reflecting
    training data skewed toward formal, professional contexts.
  </p>
  <div class="key-finding warning">
    <div class="finding-label">Takeaway</div>
    Temporal localization &mdash; the hardest task &mdash; shows the largest demographic
    disparities. Fairness gaps are widest exactly where model performance matters most.
  </div>


  <!-- ═══════════ ERROR ANALYSIS ═══════════ -->
  <h2 id="error-analysis">Error Analysis</h2>

  <p>
    To understand <em>why</em> temporal localization is so challenging, we categorized prediction
    errors into four distinct failure modes:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Relative-to-Absolute Mismatch</strong> &mdash; The model outputs timestamps as if
      each segment starts at 0s, ignoring its position in the full video. This is UniMoE-2.0&rsquo;s
      dominant failure mode (39.4% of errors).</li>
    <li><strong>Hallucination</strong> &mdash; Out-of-bounds or malformed timestamps.
      MiniCPM-o-2.6 at 26.8%.</li>
    <li><strong>Timing Shifts</strong> &mdash; Correct neighborhood, wrong interval. Dominates
      Qwen3-Omni (67%) and VITA-1.5 (75%).</li>
    <li><strong>Wrong Event, Right Time</strong> &mdash; Rare (&lt;4%), indicating semantic
      confusion is far less common than temporal confusion.</li>
  </ul>
  <figure>
    <iframe src="./diagrams/error-taxonomy.html" width="100%" height="380" scrolling="no" style="border: none; overflow:hidden;"></iframe>
    <figcaption>Figure 7: Error distribution per model. Hover for details.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    The relative-to-absolute mismatch is particularly revealing. It indicates that many
    open-source models lack the internal machinery to maintain temporal coherence across video
    segments &mdash; they process each chunk independently rather than as part of a continuous
    timeline. Gemini 3.0 Pro avoids this by processing full videos at 1 FPS with its larger
    context window.
  </p>


  <!-- ═══════════ CONCLUSION ═══════════ -->
  <h2 id="conclusion">Conclusion</h2>

  <p>
    SONIC-O1 provides a clear picture of where multimodal AI stands today. Models excel at
    <strong>content understanding</strong> &mdash; identifying what happens in conversations with
    high accuracy. But they struggle with <strong>temporal grounding</strong> &mdash; knowing
    precisely <em>when</em> events occur. And their performance varies <strong>systematically
    across demographic groups</strong>, raising important questions about equitable deployment
    in real-world settings.
  </p>
  <p>
    Our findings point to several directions for future work: stronger temporal reasoning beyond
    frame-level understanding, native audio-video integration rather than treating audio as
    optional, fairness-aware training to address demographic disparities, and broader multilingual
    and multicultural coverage.
  </p>
  <p>
    We release the full evaluation suite &mdash; dataset, evaluation scripts, and an interactive
    leaderboard &mdash; as an open, living benchmark for the research community.
  </p>

